Here's a complete example of an MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class (`WeatherTools.cs`)

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetWeather(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Unit: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/weather?q={city}&units={units}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a 5-day weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/forecast?q={city}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.openweathermap.org");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` seamlessly:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static `[McpServerToolType]` classes are resolved from DI. Use primary constructors to receive services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. |
| **Method parameter injection** | Any parameter whose type is registered in DI (and isn't annotated with `[Description]`) is injected automatically — it won't appear in the tool's JSON schema. |
| **Auto-excluded types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and DI services are all invisible to the LLM — only `[Description]`-annotated parameters become schema properties. |

The key insight: the SDK inspects each method parameter — if its type is resolvable from the DI container (or is a known framework type), it's injected at call time. Otherwise it's treated as a user-facing tool argument and must have `[Description]`.

---

## Quick Start

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
# Replace the generated files with the code above
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server starts listening on stdin for JSON-RPC messages. Connect it from VS Code, GitHub Copilot, or test with `npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector`.
